WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:05.080
Love it or hate it, it sure feels like Star
Wars: The Rise of Skywalker set out to erase

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:08.500
some things about Episode VIII and not everyone
is happy about it.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:13.240
From backstory twists to forgotten characters,
here are all the ways The Rise of Skywalker

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:14.580
ignores The Last Jedi.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:19.740
Introduced in The Force Awakens, Snoke was
one of the most mysterious characters of the

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:20.360
sequel trilogy.

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:23.820
That's why fans were so surprised when he
met an end during The Last Jedi.

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:28.340
As Rey is brought to face the Supreme Leader
of the First Order, Kylo Ren suddenly kills

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:32.180
his boss before decimating a room full of
underlings with Rey's assistance.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:36.090
The scene is one of the standout sequences
of The Last Jedi as a whole, thanks to some

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:38.220
stunning cinematography and set design.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:42.890
Still, some fans were dismayed to see Snoke
go up in smoke so soon without ever learning

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:43.620
his history.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:48.020
J.J. Abrams and company, unwilling to let
sleeping Snokes lie, resolved that mystery

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:49.780
at the start of The Rise of Skywalker.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:54.520
As Kylo Ren visits Emperor Palpatine in his
secret lair, the camera pans over a tank full

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:58.090
of floating, sleeping Snokes, and that's when
Palpatine breaks the news.

00:00:58.090 --> 00:00:59.920
He created and engineered Snoke.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:04.110
The Supreme Leader was nothing more than a
puppet, putting to rest Snoke's previous claim

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.460
that he created the connection between Rey
and Ren.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:11.620
In the grand tradition of his grandfather,
Darth Vader, Kylo Ren sported a sleek, black

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:15.340
and silver helmet meant to evoke one of the
most famous bad guys of all time.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:19.280
However, during The Last Jedi, when Supreme
Leader Snoke basically tells him that his

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:21.910
headgear looks stupid, Kylo destroys the helmet.

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:26.860
In a pretty shameless move, Abrams reverses
course on the helmet entirely, going so far

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:30.540
as to get Kylo and his crew to basically glue
the helmet back together.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:34.770
Though Kylo later abandons the helmet once
he changes course to become Ben Solo once

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:38.880
again, it's still restored as a motif after
being an important factor in the first film.

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:43.360
Obviously, Abrams didn't agree with some of
the choices that Rian Johnson made during

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:47.830
The Last Jedi, and the helmet was just one
of the smaller course corrections Abrams made

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:50.760
to a scene from The Last Jedi that didn’t
sit well with fans.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:56.380
Introduced in The Last Jedi, Vice Admiral
Holdo is chosen to aid and later replace General

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:56.880
Leia Organa.

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:01.880
Holdo's plan is to evacuate as many Resistance
allies and fighters as possible using smaller

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:06.060
transports, and though Poe thinks her plan
is way too timid to work, she has one last

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:06.900
trick up her sleeve.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:12.340
At the last minute, Holdo remains on the Resistance's
main ship alone, using it as a decoy and sacrificing

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:14.140
herself to save an entire cause.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:18.061
One might think that Holdo's incredible act
might warrant some sort of fanfare, but The

00:02:18.061 --> 00:02:21.120
Rise of Skywalker does away with this notion
pretty quickly.

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:25.740
When discussing the best tactics for attacking
the still-enormous First Order, it's suggested

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:30.050
that the Resistance could use a "Holdo maneuver,"
an idea which is shut down almost immediately

00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:31.740
because it couldn't possibly work.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:36.390
It was one of the most controversial scenes
in The Last Jedi and Abrams obviously came

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:40.550
down on the side of those criticizing the
maneuver - in a sense justifying those that

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:41.040
hated it.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:46.660
One of the biggest - and probably least expected
- corrections in The Rise of Skywalker is

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:48.540
the afterthought that is Rose Tico.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:53.360
A scrappy mechanic driven by revenge, Rose
proves to be an invaluable ally to Finn as

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:55.720
they try to escape the clutches of the First
Order.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.800
During The Last Jedi’s final battle, she's
right there on the front lines with Poe and

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:00.300
Finn.

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:05.040
After saving Finn from a suicide mission in
his speeder, the two even share a kiss, which

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:07.380
the film built to throughout their brief relationship.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:11.820
After The Last Jedi was released, actress
Kelly Marie Tran endured some of the cruelest

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:14.240
and worst abuse at the hands of Star Wars
fans.

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:18.560
Unfortunately, Abrams seems to have validated
those opinions in The Rise of Skywalker.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:23.040
Rose is relegated to the background for the
entire film, staying away from important missions

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:26.760
for no reason at all and basically serving
as a glorified extra.

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:31.060
Considering what Tran went through over her
role, quite the shock that The Rise of Skywalker

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:34.360
simply saw fit to cut her character out of
the action all together.

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:39.320
The Rise of Skywalker co-writer Chris Terrio
claims that most of Tran’s scenes were shared

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:44.160
with the late Carrie Fisher, and due to technical
issues re-inserting Leia back into the movie,

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:45.160
those scenes were cut.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:50.379
Fans were amazed when, during the climax of
The Last Jedi, Kylo Ren reveals to Rey that

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:53.769
her parents were just a couple of drunks who
sold her off for petty cash.

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:58.020
In perhaps the boldest move in the entire
sequel trilogy, Johnson tried to present a

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:01.810
Star Wars universe in which you don't have
to come from a famous family to be skilled

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:02.540
in the Force.

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:05.440
"They're buried in a pauper's desert in Jakku.

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:08.020
You have no place in this story.

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:09.160
You come from nothing.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:10.160
You're nothing."

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:15.640
Despite Johnson's best efforts, Abrams majorly
walked back this decision in The Rise of Skywalker.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:19.940
In another confession, Kylo tells Rey that
her parents were simply pretending to be nobody.

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:24.979
In fact, her father was the son of Palpatine,
and her parents sacrificed themselves to save

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:26.780
her in a very Harry Potter-esque twist.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:32.219
Johnson swung huge with his decision to make
Rey independent of any notable Star Wars lineage.

00:04:32.219 --> 00:04:36.870
In all the retcons in The Rise Of Skywalker,
this is perhaps the biggest, because it completely

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:41.509
undoes the entire theme of The Last Jedi - if
you loved The Last Jedi, this is the one that

00:04:41.509 --> 00:04:42.900
probably gets you the most.

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:48.520
After Kylo Ren and Rey decimate Snoke's remaining
forces in The Last Jedi, he offers her his

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:50.889
hand, saying they can rule the galaxy forever.

00:04:50.889 --> 00:04:55.979
Rey refuses, and the two engage in a brutal
Force tug-of-war that ends up destroying Luke

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:58.400
Skywalker's legendary lightsaber, seemingly
for good.

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:03.380
While it's implied that Leia fixed the lightsaber
between films, nobody ever addresses its destruction

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:06.860
in The Rise of Skywalker, and it simply reappears
in the narrative.

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:11.229
The more obvious Last Jedi snub, however,
comes when Rey returns to Ahch-To to run away

00:05:11.229 --> 00:05:13.160
from her Jedi powers just like Luke did.

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:17.720
As she prepares to throw her saber into the
flames, Luke's Force ghost appears and admonishes

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:20.440
her, saying she should treat the weapon with
more respect.

00:05:20.749 --> 00:05:25.000
That serves as a very obvious reference to
the moment in The Last Jedi where Luke tosses

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.440
his own saber over his shoulder dismissively.

